The plan is reviewable as a direction but not as a build brief: two of its promises contradict the repo as it stands, the install mechanism it relies on does not exist, and the skill itself (piece 3) has no test at all. Details below, with file references. Everything here comes from reading the files; I ran no commands this session.

## Breaks on contact with the repo

- **Criterion 6 ("no existing test assertion changes") cannot hold.** Adding any pack changes pinned sets: the folder list and `IDS` at `packs/review-gate/tests/review.packs.test.mjs:14` and `:32`, the SKILL.md count of 12 at `:190`, `:308`, `:345`, the catalog order string at `tests/packs-server.mjs:112`, and the card count of 7 at `tests/packs-ui.mjs:60`, `:231`, `:252`. The flag plan at `builds/flag-skill/plan.md:77` had to do exactly this and budgeted an own test-only commit. This plan needs the same section, or the builder will either fail the suite or loosen a test.
- **"Install creates the style file; uninstall keeps it" has no installer path.** The kinds are `skill | instructions | guard | push-gate | codex-log-access | worktree` (`lib/packs.js:43`). A `skill` part copies files into the skill folder and uninstall restores every recorded file (`lib/packs.js:631`). Nothing writes under `~/.agent-config-studio/` from a manifest except `codex-log-access`. So the plan silently requires a new kind in `lib/packs.js`, plus the manifest validator, `KINDS` at `review.packs.test.mjs:15`, the `kind` list in `packs/README.md:75`, and the status and update paths. That is a risk-area change the plan calls "one config file". The cheap alternative the flag pack already uses is lazy creation on first run of the helper (`flag.mjs:575`), with `style.example.json` shipped in the skill folder. Pick one and say so.
- **The helper is not "read-only" if it creates the style file**, and from Codex it will hit the workspace-write sandbox the same way flag did (`packs/flag/pack.json:65`, exit 3 handling at `flag.mjs:584`). The plan has no answer for Codex.
- **`gh` is not a requirement the manifest can express** (`requirements()` at `lib/packs.js:177` knows node, git, model-id, ask-model, CLIs, packs). The skill's last step fails with no earlier warning. Either check at run time with a plain message, or state the limitation in `explain`.
- **A review gate that is installed but has not closed HEAD is not "no review".** `--summary` throws "No complete close covers HEAD" (`review.mjs:4038`) and `--tier-json` exits 3 with `covered: false` (`review.mjs:4047`). The plan only distinguishes installed from not installed. That state is the common one mid-build and the body would say "not reviewed" for a branch that has a review in progress. Stub-based testing (criterion 1) will not catch it because the stub decides the contract.

## Underspecified in ways that make the tests tautological

- **Base detection is unspecified**, and it decides every fact. CLAUDE.md allows trunks on `main` or `staging` and stacked PRs. If base is hardcoded to `main`, a stacked PR's description lists the lower PR's commits and files. Two-dot vs three-dot diff is also unstated; after "merge main into the branch", `git diff main HEAD` reports main's own changes reversed. Specify: `--base` flag, else the open PR's base from `gh`, else the tracking branch, and three-dot diffs with `--no-merges`.
- **"Files grouped by purpose" has no classification table.** Is `pack.json` config or code? Is `SKILL.md` docs? Is `tests/fixtures/x.json` tests? Without globs in the plan, criterion 1 passes on whatever the builder decided, including a wrong grouping. Same for "behavior files" in the docs check.
- **`--check` has no defined heading syntax.** It must find sections "in order" and count words, but the plan never says whether a section is `## Title`, `**Title**`, or the configured `title` string. Word counts over code blocks, tables and the folded record are undefined. Either the check is loose enough to pass a bad body or strict enough to fail a good one.
- **The `deferred` title is user-editable.** Sections carry `title`. The review-gate rule requires a section titled exactly "Deferred findings" (`packs/review-gate/instructions/review-gate.md:8`), and bug-chasing searches that literal (`gh pr list --search "Deferred findings in:body"`). The plan pins `on` for that section but not its title. Pin the title too, and keep the section outside the `<details>` fold.
- **The "plain tone" rewrite of findings loses the ids and scores.** `--deferred` cross-references finding ids like `f2 LOW 5/100 (likelihood 1/10 × impact 5/10)` (`review.mjs:3767`), and CLAUDE.md requires every finding to show its numbers. The plan should say the deferred lines are copied verbatim from `--summary`, with plain words allowed only around them.
- **No re-run path.** The skill only knows `gh pr create`. The orchestrator opens the PR, runs fix rounds, pushes again; the body then carries a stale `HEAD:` line and a stale tier. Add `gh pr edit --body-file` when a PR already exists.
- **"Say not reviewed" versus the push gate.** In an opted-in repo the push gate blocks an unreviewed push (`review.mjs:4093`), so "not reviewed" can only occur in a non-gated repo. Fine, but the skill text should say which case it is, or a reader assumes the gate was skipped.

## Acceptance bar too weak

- **Piece 3 has zero tests.** Criteria 1 to 4 test the helper, 5 the pack. Nothing pins the skill text: run the helper, run `--check` before `gh pr create`, deferred HIGHs above the summary, "never invent facts". The repo pins skill text elsewhere (`review.close.test.mjs:960`, `review.packs.test.mjs:245`); do the same here.
- **Two helper copies with no identity pin.** Flag pins its twins byte-identical (`tests/packs-flag.mjs:21`) and each twin's SKILL.md uses its own helper path (`packs/flag/skills/codex/flag/SKILL.md:16`). The plan says "twins" and nothing more.
- **"Never invent facts" is untestable as written.** The one mechanical version is: `--check` rejects any sha, finding id, or file path in the body that is not in the facts JSON. Without that, the only guard is prose in the skill.
- **Warnings go where?** Criterion 2 says "warning, kept on" for `deferred`, but the helper prints one JSON object. A stderr warning the model may never read. Put warnings in the JSON.
- **Criterion 1's "stub review.mjs"** lets the builder define the contract. At least one case should run the real `review.mjs` from `packs/review-gate/runtime/` against a temp log, as the review-gate tests do.

## Over-built for one user's PR descriptions

- **GIFs, `tone`, `footer`, per-section `maxWords`, emoji toggles** are five style axes for a user who edits JSON by hand. GIF-by-branch-hash and the "empty list, with a note" case are tests for a feature with no evidence anyone wants it yet. Sections on/off and order are the whole value; the rest can arrive when asked for.
- **`--check` enforcing word limits at the end** turns a style preference into a retry loop for the model. Enforce only invariants that matter for correctness: Deferred findings present and verbatim, record folded, no unknown facts. Word limits belong in the skill's instructions, not the gate.
- **A `tier` section** duplicates the tier line that `--summary` already prints (`review.mjs:2820`).

Two smaller items: `lib/packs.js:41` `ORDER` does not list the new pack, so it sorts last by name, which is fine but should be a decision, and the style example must pass the C3 sweep at `tests/packs-catalog.mjs:19` (no `/Users/`, no names), so ship `gif.urls` empty.
